mosphere, not for determining advancement.\n\n---\n\n**4) Is there a golden buzzer being pressed or confetti falling?**  \n❌ **No.** There is no visual evidence of a golden buzzer being pressed or confetti falling. This implies the show is in a standard elimination round, not a special “golden buzzer” moment.\n\n---\n\n**5) Are judges giving feedback or making decisions?**  \n✅ **Yes.** Judges are shown interacting with contestants (frames 6, 8–9, 11–14) and reacting emotionally. In frame 15, the lit X’s indicate a decision has been made — all judges voted to eliminate the act. Simon Cowell (frame 8) is shown with a contemplative expression, suggesting evaluation. The female judge in the sparkly dress (frames 11–14) is seen speaking to the contestant — likely giving feedback.\n\n---\n\n**6) Are there large screens showing audience vote counts or results?**  \n❌ **No.** The large screens (frames 19–20, 28–30) show the stage and judges’ desk, but **no vote counts, percentages, or audience tally results are displayed.** This further supports that advancement is **not determined by audience votes.**\n\n---\n\n**Additional Context:**\n\n- The format is consistent with *Got Talent* franchise shows: contestants perform live on stage, judges have “X” buzzers to eliminate acts, and advancement is based on judges’ decisions (especially in early rounds).\n- The presence of the “X” buzzers and lack of audience voting mechanisms strongly indicates that **judges alone decide advancement**.\n- The show is live (audience is present and reacting in real time), and contestants are performing on stage — satisfying the condition that “as long as the show is live, and they can advance if their performance impresses the judges.”\n\n---\n\n✅ **Conclusion:** The talent show format depicted is one where **contestants perform live on stage, and advancement is determined solely by judges’ decisions (not audience votes)**. Therefore, **answer '2' (choice B) is correct.**"}, {'batch_start': 60,